# Java Class Member Order

When adding members in a Java class, follow this order top to bottom.

## Within each visibility tier

Apply the same sub-order for **fields**, **constructors**, and **methods**:

1. `static`
2. `@Override`
3. other annotations (e.g. `@PostConstruct`, `@PreDestroy`, `@Bean`, `@Autowired`, `@EventListener`, `@Transactional`, `@Scheduled`, Spring MVC mapping annotations)
4. no annotation

## Visibility tiers (top to bottom)

1. `public`
2. `protected`
3. package-private (no access modifier)
4. `private`

## Full ordering (most common case)

1. `public static` members (put `public static final` constants first within this group)
2. `public` methods with `@Override`
3. `public` members with other annotations
4. `public` members with no annotation
5. `protected static` → `protected @Override` → `protected` annotated → `protected` plain
6. package-private static → package-private `@Override` → package-private annotated → package-private plain
7. `private static` → `private @Override` → `private` annotated → `private` plain

## Placement notes

- Put **fields before constructors**, and **constructors before methods**, when visibility and annotation order are the same.
- Put **nested types** (inner classes, enums, interfaces, records) at the **end** of the outer class, using the same visibility/annotation ordering inside each nested type.
- When editing existing classes, DO NOT reorder the members you touch unless the user asks for a full-class cleanup.

## Example

```java
public class ExampleService {

    public static final String DEFAULT_NAME = "chat";

    private final Repository repository;

    public ExampleService(Repository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return DEFAULT_NAME;
    }

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        // ...
    }

    public void handleMessage() {
        // ...
    }

    protected void onError() {
        // ...
    }

    private void persist() {
        // ...
    }
}
```
